WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.219
Unsloth, and how to read speed claims.

2
00:00:02.499 --> 00:00:14.189
Unsloth is an open-source fine-tuning library that TRL’s docs list as an integration, and Maxime Labonne’s course includes a Colab notebook that fine-tunes Llama 3.

3
00:00:14.469 --> 00:00:15.387
1 with it.

4
00:00:15.667 --> 00:00:20.829
Its documentation claims about 2 times faster training with 70 percent less VRAM.

5
00:00:21.109 --> 00:00:29.067
Its fine-tuning guide says local installation requires Windows or Linux, so on a Mac, MLX-LM is the native choice.

6
00:00:29.347 --> 00:00:35.811
Treat speed and memory numbers from any vendor or library as claims about their benchmark, not your run.

7
00:00:36.091 --> 00:00:45.456
The check is cheap: run the same data, model, rank, and sequence length in both stacks, and record tokens per second, peak memory, and your task metric.

8
00:00:45.736 --> 00:00:50.792
If the faster stack also changes the result, find out why before trusting either.

9
00:00:51.072 --> 00:01:00.096
The same applies to every claim in this lesson about defaults: they were read from the docs and source on the review date, and fine-tuning libraries change quickly.

10
00:01:00.376 --> 00:01:10.253
Pin the versions you used, and when you upgrade, re-run a short reference training job and compare its scores with the last known good run before trusting the new setup.
